                <pb facs="1044" n="1036" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg1036"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.00/710b: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-06-14T16:00:00-04:00">June 14, 1938—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>368. Princeton University, which is operating an archeological expedition in the
                    Sanjak of Alexandretta on behalf of a group of <pb facs="1045" n="1037"
                        xml:id="pg1037"/> American institutions, has received indications that the
                    work of its expedition and one operating in the same area under the direction of
                    the Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago may be jeopardized by the
                    current Franco-Turkish negotiations concerning the Sanjak. The rights of these
                    American institutions would appear to be safeguarded fully by Article 14 of the
                    Syrian Mandate to the benefits of which the United States is entitled under the
                    American-French Convention of April 4, 1924. Please make inquiry at the Foreign
                    Office and endeavor to obtain assurances that the rights of these institutions
                    will not be adversely affected by any decisions which may be reached with
                    respect to the Sanjak.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
